WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.600
Question 0071: a duel of three wizards,
a senior-level question.

2
00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:09.000
Three parts: I go first, I choose my turn,
and the general case.

3
00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:15.200
Three wizards cast spells in turns:
wizard 1, then 2, then 3, and around again.

4
00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:20.700
On his turn each wizard targets one rival
and hits with his chance. Casting into the air is allowed.

5
00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:26.700
The cat rule: if all three are alive
after the first round, all become cats and lose.

6
00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:32.700
The wand: I choose my hit chance in advance,
and everyone sees it. Everyone plays optimally.

7
00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:37.600
Part A: I cast first,
against wizards of 0.6 and 0.8.

8
00:00:38.300 --> 00:00:43.533
Wizard 2 hits with 0.6 and wizard 3 with 0.8.
The only variable is my wand, p₁.

9
00:00:43.533 --> 00:00:49.033
The idea: solve from the end backward.
First the duel, then wizard 3, wizard 2, and me.

10
00:00:49.833 --> 00:00:55.333
Step 1: when two are left, they shoot in turn.
The opener hits at once, or both miss,

11
00:00:55.333 --> 00:01:00.733
and we are back in the same state. Solving gives
the opener's chance to win, V(x,y).

12
00:01:00.733 --> 00:01:05.767
Wizard 2 opening against wizard 3 wins 65%:
the first shot is worth a lot.

13
00:01:05.767 --> 00:01:09.767
And whoever hit in the first round is second in the duel,
because the turn passes to the one left.

14
00:01:10.567 --> 00:01:14.733
Step 2: wizard 3 must cast,
or everyone becomes a cat. At whom?

15
00:01:14.733 --> 00:01:18.733
If he hits me, he is second against wizard 2.
If he hits wizard 2, he is second against me.

16
00:01:18.733 --> 00:01:23.967
Compare the two chances and clear
the denominators: a simple inequality in p₁.

17
00:01:23.967 --> 00:01:29.267
He targets me exactly when p₁ > 0.6,
that is, the stronger of me and wizard 2.

18
00:01:29.767 --> 00:01:35.267
Step 3: wizard 2. When p₁ < 0.6, wizard 3
targets him, so the air loses: he targets 3.

19
00:01:35.267 --> 00:01:40.767
When p₁ > 0.6, in the air wizard 3 targets me,
and wizard 2 then opens against him: 12/23.

20
00:01:40.767 --> 00:01:46.267
For hitting wizard 3 to pay, he would need
p₁ < 0.355. A contradiction, so he casts into the air.

21
00:01:46.767 --> 00:01:52.267
Step 4: me. If I cast, I target wizard 3,
the stronger. But should I cast at all?

22
00:01:52.767 --> 00:01:58.267
If p₁ < 0.6, nobody targets me.
I cast into the air and open against whoever is left.

23
00:01:58.267 --> 00:02:03.767
Wizard 2 hits 3 with chance 0.6. Otherwise
wizard 3 hits 2, with chance 0.4 times 0.8.

24
00:02:03.767 --> 00:02:09.267
If p₁ > 0.6, wizard 3 targets me. I must
hit at once, and get at most 0.4.

25
00:02:10.067 --> 00:02:15.867
My chance by wand: it rises until just
below 0.6, and drops once I pass wizard 2.

26
00:02:15.867 --> 00:02:20.767
Just below 0.6: 513/805, almost 64%.
A sniper, wand 1, gets only 40%.

27
00:02:20.767 --> 00:02:24.767
Why not exactly 0.6? There wizard 3 is indifferent
and might pick me. So slightly below, say 0.59.

28
00:02:25.567 --> 00:02:30.800
The page's table: up to 0.6 I cast into the air,
and above it I must target wizard 3.

29
00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:35.400
Above 0.6 the chance drops to 0.223,
and climbs only to 0.4 with a sniper.

30
00:02:36.200 --> 00:02:41.433
The principle for both: kill the stronger,
to face the weaker, me, at the end.

31
00:02:42.233 --> 00:02:47.333
Now the whole game as a Markov chain:
a state is who is alive and whose turn it is.

32
00:02:47.833 --> 00:02:53.533
The top row is the first round: I cast into the air,
wizard 2 at 3, and if he missed, wizard 3 at 2.

33
00:02:53.533 --> 00:02:58.200
A hit leads to a duel, where they cast
in turn until someone hits.

34
00:02:58.200 --> 00:03:03.500
The bottom row is the wins. The chance of each end
is a sum over the paths into it.

35
00:03:03.500 --> 00:03:09.000
Duel 1 vs 2, my turn: I hit with 0.6,
else wizard 2 casts, and if he missed, me again.

36
00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:12.600
This is the loop of V: from this state
I win with 5/7.

37
00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:17.833
And duel 1 vs 3, my turn: we reach it
with 0.32, and I win it with 15/23.

38
00:03:17.833 --> 00:03:21.567
Each path is the product of the chances
on its arrows.

39
00:03:21.567 --> 00:03:25.567
Cats: both miss,
0.4 times 0.2, that is 8%.

40
00:03:25.567 --> 00:03:30.300
Me: 0.6 times 5/7, plus 0.32 times 15/23.
In total 63.7%.

41
00:03:30.300 --> 00:03:35.033
Wizard 2: hits 3 and wins as second against me,
0.6 times 2/7, about 17.1%.

42
00:03:35.033 --> 00:03:39.833
Wizard 3: 0.32 times 8/23, about 11.1%.
The strongest of all gets the least.

43
00:03:39.833 --> 00:03:43.800
The check: 63.7 plus 17.1, 11.1 and 8,
exactly 100%.

44
00:03:44.600 --> 00:03:49.333
In the page's box: me first with 0.599,
and the Markov chain of the choice.

45
00:03:49.333 --> 00:03:53.500
Wand 0.8: wizard 3 targets me,
and wizard 2 casts into the air.

46
00:03:53.500 --> 00:03:57.967
A sniper: I hit wizard 3 at once,
and wizard 2 opens against me. Only 40%.

47
00:03:57.967 --> 00:04:01.000
And back just below 0.6:
63.7% again.

48
00:04:01.800 --> 00:04:07.300
The trap: thinking the strongest wand is best.
Here it gives 40%, against 63.7% for the weak one.

49
00:04:08.100 --> 00:04:13.000
Part B: now I also choose my turn,
and the order of the two rivals.

50
00:04:13.700 --> 00:04:19.200
Three turns, two orders in each:
six orders. Each is solved like part A.

51
00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:24.700
In each, the best wand comes out just below
0.6: be the weakest, so nobody targets me.

52
00:04:24.700 --> 00:04:29.433
First or second: 63.7% or 60.7%,
depending on which rival casts first.

53
00:04:29.433 --> 00:04:34.900
Third after 0.6 then 0.8: 65.1%, the best.
After 0.8 then 0.6, only 55.6%.

54
00:04:35.700 --> 00:04:41.200
Let's work out the winning order. I am wizard 3, with p₃.
Wizard 1 hits with 0.6, and wizard 2 with 0.8.

55
00:04:41.200 --> 00:04:46.600
The duels of this order, with the same formula:
I open, or the rival opens against me.

56
00:04:47.100 --> 00:04:52.400
I am last in the round: I must cast, at the stronger,
wizard 2, to stay against the weaker.

57
00:04:52.400 --> 00:04:57.900
Wizard 2: in the air I kill him. So he casts,
at the stronger of wizard 1 and me: at 1.

58
00:04:58.700 --> 00:05:04.500
Wizard 1: hitting wizard 2 beats the air
as long as p₃ < 5/6. So he also targets 2.

59
00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:10.400
My chance: three ways into a duel.
The last one, my own hit, is just a backup.

60
00:05:10.400 --> 00:05:13.933
In the limit just below 0.6: 13101/20125,
about 65.1%.

61
00:05:14.733 --> 00:05:20.333
The chain of this order: wizard 1 at 2,
if he missed wizard 2 at 1, and if he missed, me.

62
00:05:20.333 --> 00:05:24.033
The duels: in all of them I play
against whoever is left.

63
00:05:24.033 --> 00:05:28.000
At the end: me 65.1%, wizard 1 20.6%,
wizard 2 11.1%.

64
00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:33.300
Cats only if all three of us miss:
0.4 times 0.2 times 0.4, that is 3.2%.

65
00:05:33.300 --> 00:05:38.800
And wizard 1: hits wizard 2 and wins as second against me,
or opens against me after my hit.

66
00:05:38.800 --> 00:05:43.200
And me: three ways into a duel,
and in the first two I open.

67
00:05:43.200 --> 00:05:48.700
Wizard 2: only if he hit wizard 1, and then he is
second against me: 0.32 times 8/23, about 11.1%.

68
00:05:50.100 --> 00:05:55.600
Why does this win? When I am last, both rivals
cast before me, and the cats drop to 3.2%.

69
00:05:55.600 --> 00:06:00.900
And the weaker casts first: he targets 0.8,
and I stay against 0.6, not 0.8.

70
00:06:01.700 --> 00:06:06.000
In the box, the "best choice" button
goes through the six orders.

71
00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:10.733
It picks third, after 0.6 then 0.8,
just below 0.6: 65.1%.

72
00:06:11.333 --> 00:06:15.000
And the Markov chain of this choice,
with the same numbers.

73
00:06:15.800 --> 00:06:19.900
Part C: any rivals, a < b.
What is the best choice?

74
00:06:20.600 --> 00:06:26.100
What we saw: each wizard targets the stronger
of the other two. Nobody targets the weakest.

75
00:06:26.100 --> 00:06:31.567
So there are two plans: hide as the weakest,
or be a sniper and kill the strongest first.

76
00:06:31.567 --> 00:06:37.067
Sniper first: wand 1, and I kill b at once.
Rival a opens against me, and wins only if he hits.

77
00:06:37.067 --> 00:06:42.300
Hide: third, after a then b,
with a wand just below a, as in part B.

78
00:06:42.300 --> 00:06:46.300
A sniper second or third gets no more:
whoever casts before him sees him as the strongest.

79
00:06:47.100 --> 00:06:52.400
In the hiding plan, as in part B: I am
last and target b, and b targets a.

80
00:06:52.400 --> 00:06:56.200
And a targets b, since 1 − a is always
greater than (1 − b)a.

81
00:06:57.000 --> 00:07:01.800
My chance: a sum over the three paths
of the chain, as in part B.

82
00:07:01.800 --> 00:07:06.900
Substitute V from step 1. Against an equal rival,
the fraction reduces to 1/(2 − a).

83
00:07:06.900 --> 00:07:10.800
And against b: a over a + b − ab.
Nothing to reduce here.

84
00:07:10.800 --> 00:07:14.700
And this is the general expression of my chance,
in a and b only.

85
00:07:15.500 --> 00:07:19.533
In the same way, the chances of the two rivals
and of the cats.

86
00:07:19.533 --> 00:07:25.033
The cats: all three miss, since my wand
is close to a: (1 − a)² times (1 − b).

87
00:07:25.533 --> 00:07:31.033
Check: a = 0.6 and b = 0.8 give
13101/20125, exactly the answer of part B.

88
00:07:31.033 --> 00:07:35.033
These are limits: a wand just below a
gets as close to them as you like.

89
00:07:35.833 --> 00:07:40.000
Hide when this expression is greater than 1 − a,
the sniper's chance.

90
00:07:40.000 --> 00:07:44.533
The first term alone beats 1 − a
when a > 2 − √2, about 0.586.

91
00:07:45.033 --> 00:07:50.533
With the other two terms the threshold a* is lower,
and depends a little on b: between 0.456 and 0.478.

92
00:07:50.533 --> 00:07:55.933
Below the threshold, sniper first. Above it, hide.
In part B, a = 0.6, so we hide.

93
00:07:56.733 --> 00:08:01.833
The rule on a few pairs of rivals: each row
has the chance of each plan, and the choice.

94
00:08:01.833 --> 00:08:06.933
0.4 and 0.8 are below the threshold: sniper, 60%.
0.47 and 0.9 are already above it: hide.

95
00:08:06.933 --> 00:08:12.433
0.47 and 0.55: the threshold rises as b shrinks,
and the sniper barely wins. And 0.7 and 0.9: 73.8%.

96
00:08:13.233 --> 00:08:16.733
In the box: rivals 0.4 and 0.8,
below the threshold.

97
00:08:16.733 --> 00:08:21.033
The best choice: sniper first, 60%,
against 48.6% when hiding.

98
00:08:21.033 --> 00:08:24.500
And now rivals 0.7 and 0.9,
above the threshold.

99
00:08:24.500 --> 00:08:28.033
The choice: third, just below 0.7,
with 73.8%.

100
00:08:28.833 --> 00:08:35.233
In short: be the weakest, cast last,
and let the strong ones fight each other.

101
00:08:35.233 --> 00:08:41.133
Try your own rivals in the box
on the page of question 0071 on the site.
